          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The
            %em cardinal numbers
            (or just
            = succeed ")" do
              %em cardinals
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+number"} natural numbers
            to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/numbers"} numbers
            of possibly infinite magnitudes. Specifically, cardinal numbers generalise the concept of ‘the number of …’. In particular, the number of natural numbers is the first infinite cardinal number.
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            Naïvely, a
            %strong cardinal number
            should be an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
            class of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets"} sets
            %strong cardinality
            of a set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            would be its isomorphism class. That is:
          %ol
            %li every set has a unique cardinal number as its cardinality;
            %li every cardinal number is the cardinality of some set;
            %li two sets have the same cardinality if and only if they are isomorphic as sets.
          %p
            Then a
            %strong finite cardinal
            is the cardinality of a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+set"} finite set
            %strong infinite cardinal
            or
            %strong transfinite cardinal
            is the cardinality of an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinite+set"} infinite set
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
          %p
            Taking this definition literally in material
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set+theory"} set theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proper+class"} proper class
            (so one could not make further sets using them as elements). For this reason, in axiomatic set theory one usually defines a cardinal number as a particular representative of this
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+class"} equivalence class
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                The
                %strong cardinality
                of a set
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
                is the smallest possible ordinal rank of any
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/well-order"} well-order
                on
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi S
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinal+number"} ordinal number
                (usually defined following von Neumann) which can be put in bijection with
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi S
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
                %strong cardinal number
                is then any cardinality, i.e. any ordinal which is not in bijection with any smaller ordinal.
              %ul
                %li
                  %p
                    On well-orderable sets, this cardinality function satisfies (1–3), but one needs the
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
                    (precisely, the
                    = succeed ")" do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/well-ordering+theorem"} well-ordering theorem
                %li
                  %p
                    In the absence of
                    = succeed "," do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/excluded+middle"} excluded middle
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi S
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
                    is as the set of all
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinal+numbers"} ordinal numbers
                    less than the ordinal rank of every
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/well-order"} well-order
                    on
                    = succeed "." do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mi S
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            %li
              %p
                Alternatively, we can define the
                %strong cardinality
                of a set
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                to be the set of all well-founded
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pure+sets"} pure sets
                that are isomorphic as sets to
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                and such that no pure set of smaller hereditary rank (that is, which occurs earlier in the
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/von+Neumann+hierarchy"} von Neumann hierarchy
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              %ul
                %li
                  On those sets that are isomorphic to some well-founded set, this cardinality function satisfies (1–3), but one needs some assumption to prove that every set is isomorphic to a well-founded set. (This will follow directly from the
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+foundation"} axiom of foundation
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
          %p
            In the absence of the appropriate axioms, the definitions above can still be used to define
            %strong well-ordered cardinals
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %strong well-founded cardinals
          %p
            From the perspective of structural
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set+theory"} set theory
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principle+of+equivalence"} principle of equivalence
          %ul
            %li
              A
              %strong cardinal
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
              (that is, an object of
              = succeed ")." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
          %p
            However, one still may need sets of cardinals, that is sets that serve as the target of a cardinality function satisfying (1–3) on any (small) collection of sets. One can construct this as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient+set"} quotient set
            of that collection.
          %p
            Lowercase Greek letters starting from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi κ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \kappa
            are often used for cardinal numbers.
          %h3#in_homotopy_type_theory In homotopy type theory
          %p
            A
            %strong cardinal
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
            is an element of the type of cardinals
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi κ
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %msub
                    %mi Card
                    %mi 𝒰
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \kappa \colon Card_\mathcal{U}
            relative to a universe
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒰
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{U}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Set
                    %mi 𝒰
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set_\mathcal{U}
            relative to
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒰
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{U}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Card
                    %mi 𝒰
                  %mo ≔
                  %msub
                    %mrow
                      %mo |
                      %msub
                        %mi Set
                        %mi 𝒰
                      %mo |
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Card_\mathcal{U} \coloneqq \left| Set_\mathcal{U} \right|_0
            with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Set
                    %mi 𝒰
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set_\mathcal{U}
            defined as
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Set
                    %mi 𝒰
                  %mo ≔
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo :
                      %mi 𝒰
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                    %mrow
                      %mi b
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %mo :
                      %mi a
                      %msub
                        %mo =
                        %mi A
                      %mi b
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                    %mrow
                      %mi q
                      %mo :
                      %mi a
                      %msub
                        %mo =
                        %mi A
                      %mi b
                  %mi p
                  %msub
                    %mo =
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %msub
                        %mo =
                        %mi A
                      %mi b
                  %mi q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set_\mathcal{U} \coloneqq \sum_{A:\mathcal{U}} \prod_{a:A} \prod_{b:A} \prod_{p:a =_A b} \prod_{q:a =_A b} p =_{a =_A b} q
          %h2#cardinal_arithmetic Cardinal arithmetic
          %h3#definition_2 Definition
          %p
            For
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {|S|}
            for its cardinality. Then the standard operations in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
            induce
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/arithmetic"} arithmetic
            operations on cardinal numbers (“
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cardinal+arithmetic"}> cardinal arithmetic
            ”):
          %p
            For
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_1
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_2
            two sets, the
            %strong sum
            of their cardinalities is the cardinality of their
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/disjoint+union"} disjoint union
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coproduct"} coproduct
            in
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mo +
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mo ≔
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ⨿
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  {|S_1|} + {|S_2|} \coloneqq {|S_1 \amalg S_2|}
                  \\,.
          %p
            More generally, given any family
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mi i
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo :
                      %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (S_i)_{i: I}
            of sets indexed by a set
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
            %strong sum
            of their cardinalities is the cardinality of their disjoint union:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo :
                      %mi I
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mo ≔
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %munder
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo :
                        %mi I
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\sum_{i: I} {|S_i|} \coloneqq {|\coprod_{i: I} S_i|}
                  \\,.
          %p
            Likewise, the
            %strong product
            of their cardinalities is the cardinality of their
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+product"} cartesian product
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product"} product
            in
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mo ≔
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ×
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  {|S_1|} \, {|S_2|} \coloneqq {|S_1 \times S_2|}
                  \\,.
          %p
            More generally again, given any family
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mi i
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo :
                      %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (S_i)_{i: I}
            of sets indexed by a set
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
            %strong product
            of their cardinalities is the cardinality of their cartesian product:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo :
                      %mi I
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mo ≔
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %munder
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo :
                        %mi I
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\prod_{i: I} {|S_i|} \coloneqq {|\prod_{i: I} S_i|}
                  \\,.
          %p
            Also, the
            %strong exponential
            of one cardinality raised to the power of the other is the cardinality of their
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function+set"} function set
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exponential+object"} exponential object
            in
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %msub
                        %mi S
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %msub
                        %mi S
                        %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mo ≔
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi Set
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  {|S_1|}^{|S_2|} \coloneqq {|Set(S_2,S_1)|}
                  \\,.
          %p
            In particular, we have
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mn 2
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mi S
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2^{|S|}
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/excluded+middle"} excluded middle
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/power+set"} power set
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P(S)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive
            (but not
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/predicative+mathematics"} predicative
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Ω
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mi S
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega^{|S|}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega
            is the cardinality of the set of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/truth+values"} truth values
          %p
            The usual way to define an ordering on cardinal numbers is that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mo ≤
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {|S_1|} \leq {|S_2|}
            if there exists an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/injection"} injection
            from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_1
            to
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_2
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mo ≤
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo :
                  %mo ⇔
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo ∃
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ↪
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  ({|S_1|} \leq {|S_2|})
                  \\;:\Leftrightarrow\;
                  (\exists (S_1 \hookrightarrow S_2))
                  \\,.
          %p
            Classically, this is almost equivalent to the existence of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/surjection"} surjection
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mn 2
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_2 \to S_1
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_1
            is
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/empty+set"} empty
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inhabited+sets"} inhabited sets
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive mathematics
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mo ≤
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {|S_1|} \leq {|S_2|}
            if there exists a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subset"} subset
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_2
            and a surjection
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \to S_1
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_1
            (or
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/decidable+subset"} decidable subset
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_2
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/excluded+middle"} excluded middle
          %p
            This order relation is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/antisymmetric+relation"} antisymmetric
            (and therefore a
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partial+order"} partial order
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cantor%E2%80%93Schroeder%E2%80%93Bernstein+theorem"} Cantor–Schroeder–Bernstein theorem
            (proved by Cantor using the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/well-ordering+theorem"} well-ordering theorem
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ↪
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_1 \hookrightarrow S_2
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mn 2
                  %mo ↪
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_2 \hookrightarrow S_1
            exist, then a bijection
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ≅
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_1 \cong S_2
            exists. This theorem is not constructively valid, however.
          %p
            The well-ordered cardinals are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/well-order"} well-ordered
            by the ordering
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo &lt;
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lt
            on
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinal+numbers"} ordinal numbers
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi κ
                  %mo ≤
                  %mi λ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \kappa \leq \lambda
            iff
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi κ
                  %mo &lt;
                  %mi λ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \kappa \lt \lambda
            or
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi κ
                    %mo =
                    %mi λ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \kappa = \lambda
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi κ
                  %mo &lt;
                  %mi λ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \kappa \lt \lambda
            if
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi κ
                      %mo +
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi λ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \kappa^+ \leq \lambda
          %p
            The
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/successor"} successor
            of a well-ordered cardinal
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi κ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \kappa
            is the smallest well-ordered cardinal larger than
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi κ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \kappa
            = succeed "'s" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi κ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \kappa
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinal+number"} ordinal number
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hartog%27s+number"} Hartog's number
          %h3#properties Properties
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                It is traditional to write
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%84%B5"} ℵ
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mrow
                        %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {}_0
                for the first infinite cardinal (the cardinality of the
                = succeed ")," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers"} natural numbers
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi ℵ
                        %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \aleph_1
                for the next (the first uncountable cardinality), and so on. In this way every cardinal (assuming choice) is labeled
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi ℵ
                        %mi μ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \aleph_\mu
                for a unique
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinal+number"} ordinal number
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi μ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mi ℵ
                          %mi μ
                        %msup
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo +
                        %mo =
                        %msub
                          %mi ℵ
                          %mrow
                            %msup
                              %mi μ
                              %mo +
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\aleph_\mu)^+ = \aleph_{\mu^+}
            %li
              %p
                For every cardinal
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi π
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mn 2
                        %mi π
                      %mo &gt;
                      %mi π
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2^\pi \gt \pi
                (this is sometimes called
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cantor%27s+theorem"} Cantor's theorem
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mn 2
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi ℵ
                            %mn 0
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mi ℵ
                        %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2^{\aleph_0} = \aleph_{1}
                (or more generally whether
                = succeed ")" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mn 2
                          %mrow
                            %msub
                              %mi ℵ
                              %mi μ
                        %mo =
                        %msub
                          %mi ℵ
                          %mrow
                            %msup
                              %mi μ
                              %mo +
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2^{\aleph_\mu} = \aleph_{\mu^+}
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuum+hypothesis"} continuum hypothesis
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ZFC"} ZFC
            %li
              %p
                For every transfinite cardinal
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi π
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi
                we have (using the axiom of choice)
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi π
                      %mo +
                      %mi π
                      %mo =
                      %mi π
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi + \pi = \pi
                and
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi π
                        %mo ⋅
                        %mi π
                        %mo =
                        %mi π
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi \cdot \pi = \pi
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/idempotent"} idempotent
          %h2#properties_of_cardinals Properties of cardinals
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                A transfinite cardinal
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi π
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi
                is a
                %strong
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+cardinal"} regular cardinal
                if no set of cardinality
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi π
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi
                is the union of fewer than
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi π
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi
                sets of cardinality less than
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi π
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi π
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi
                is regular if given a function
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi P
                      %mo →
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P \to X
                (regarded as a family
                = succeed ")" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                        %msub
                          %mi P
                          %mi x
                        %msub
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                          %mrow
                            %mi x
                            %mo ∈
                            %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{P_x\}_{x\in X}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mi X
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mo &lt;
                      %mi π
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {|X|} \lt \pi
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %msub
                          %mi P
                          %mi x
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mo &lt;
                      %mi π
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {|P_x|} \lt \pi
                for all
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in X
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                          %mi P
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mo &lt;
                        %mi π
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {|P|} \lt \pi
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi π
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi
                is regular if the category
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Set
                        %mrow
                          %mo &lt;
                          %mi π
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Set_{\lt\pi}
                of sets of cardinality
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo &lt;
                      %mi π
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lt\pi
                has all
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimits"} colimits
                of size
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo &lt;
                        %mi π
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lt\pi
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/successor"} successor
                of any infinite cardinal, such as
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi ℵ
                          %mn 1
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \aleph_1
            %li
              %p
                A cardinal is called
                %strong singular
                if it is not regular. For instance,
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi ℵ
                        %mi ω
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋃
                        %mrow
                          %mi n
                          %mo ∈
                          %mi ℕ
                      %msub
                        %mi ℵ
                        %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \aleph_\omega = \bigcup_{n\in \mathbb{N}} \aleph_n
                is singular, more or less by definition, since
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi ℵ
                        %mi n
                      %mo &lt;
                      %msub
                        %mi ℵ
                        %mi ω
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \aleph_n \lt \aleph_\omega
                and
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                          %mi ℕ
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mo =
                        %msub
                          %mi ℵ
                          %mn 0
                        %mo &lt;
                        %msub
                          %mi ℵ
                          %mi ω
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {|\mathbb{N}|} = \aleph_0 \lt \aleph_\omega
            %li
              %p
                A
                %strong limit cardinal
                is one which is not a successor of any other cardinal. Note that
                %em every
                infinite cardinal is a limit
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinal+number"} ordinal
                (in the picture where cardinals are identified with certain ordinals).
            %li
              %p
                A
                %strong strong limit cardinal
                is a cardinal
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi π
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi
                such that if
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi λ
                        %mo &lt;
                        %mi π
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda \lt \pi
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mn 2
                          %mi λ
                        %mo &lt;
                        %mi π
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2^\lambda \lt \pi
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi λ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi λ
                          %mo +
                        %mo ≤
                        %msup
                          %mn 2
                          %mi λ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda^+ \le 2^\lambda
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuum+hypothesis"} continuum hypothesis
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mn 2
                        %mi λ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2^\lambda
                is the cardinality of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/power+set"} power set
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi P
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi λ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P(\lambda)
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi π
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi
                is a strong limit iff the category
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Set
                        %mrow
                          %mo &lt;
                          %mi π
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Set_{\lt\pi}
                is an elementary
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
            %li
              %p
                An
                %strong
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inaccessible+cardinal"} inaccessible cardinal
                is any (usually uncountable) regular strong limit cardinal. A
                %strong weakly inaccessible cardinal
                is a regular limit cardinal.
            %li
              %p
                A cardinal
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi κ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \kappa
                is a
                %strong
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measurable+cardinal"} measurable cardinal
                if some (hence any) set of cardinality
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi κ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \kappa
                admits a two-valued
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measure"} measure
                which is
                = succeed "-additive," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi κ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \kappa
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ultrafilter"} ultrafilter
                which is
                = succeed "-complete." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi κ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \kappa
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid+cardinality"} groupoid cardinality
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/counting+measure"} counting measure
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinal+number"} ordinal number
          %h2#references References
          %p The original article is
          %ul
            %li#Cantor1895
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Georg+Cantor"}> Georg Cantor
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Beitr%C3%A4ge+zur+Begr%C3%BCndung+der+transfiniten+Mengenlehre"} Beiträge zur Begründung der transfiniten Mengenlehre
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ernst+Zermelo"} Ernst Zermelo
              (ed.),
              = succeed "," do
                %em Georg Cantor – Gesammelte Abhandlungen mathematischen und philosophischen Inhalts
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://archive.org/details/contributionstof00cant"} online English translation
          %p The book
          %ul
            %li
              Peter J Cameron,
              %em Sets, Logic and Categories
              (ISBN: 1-85233-056-2 )
          %p contains a very readable account of ZFC and the definitions of both ordinal and cardinal numbers.
          %p Any serious reference on set theory should cover cardinal numbers. The long-established respected tome is
          %ul
            %li
              Thomas Jech
              = succeed ";" do
                %em
                  %a{:href => "http://books.google.com/books?id=pLxq0myANiEC"} Set Theory
          %p there are also some references listed at
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "http://mathworld.wolfram.com/CardinalNumber.html"} MathWorld
                and
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cardinal_number"}> the English Wikipedia
                \.
          %p A good introduction to infinite cardinals is:
          %ul
            %li
              Frank R. Drake,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Set Theory: An Introduction to Large Cardinals
          %p For a much deeper treatment, which assumes most of the material in Drake as a prerequisite, see:
          %ul
            %li
              Akihiro Kanamori,
              %em The Higher Infinite: Large Cardinals in Set Theory from Their Beginning, Springer, 2003.
          %p This is a very readable and freely available historical introduction:
          %ul
            %li
              Akihiro Kanamori and M. Magidor, The evolution of large cardinal axioms in set theory, in
              = succeed "," do
                %em Higher Set Theory
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://math.bu.edu/people/aki/e.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            %em Standard
            approaches start with a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/material+set+theory"} material set theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structural+set+theory"} structural set theory
            as emphasized above. Since cardinality is isomorphism-invariant, it is easy to interpret the standard material structurally, although the basic definitions will be different. There does not seem to be a standard account of cardinality from within structural set theory.
          %p
            For cardinal numbers in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
          %ul
            %li#UFP13
              Univalent Foundations Project,
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/HoTT+book"} Homotopy Type Theory – Univalent Foundations of Mathematics
              (2013)
          %p For a critical discussion of the history of the meaning of Cantor’s “Kardinalen”, see
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Lawvere"}> William Lawvere
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cohesive+Toposes+and+Cantor%27s+%22lauter+Einsen%22"} Cohesive Toposes and Cantor's "lauter Einsen"
          %p
            which argues that Cantor’s original meaning of
            %em set
            was more like what today is
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesive"} cohesive
              set
            and that his
            %em Kardinalen
            refer to the
            %em underlying set
            (see at
            = succeed ")." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flat+modality"} flat modality
